  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1211 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe next significant shortwave shown in the SW flow aloft pattern is \nwell agreed upon by the 18Z and 00Z deterministic runs with perhaps \nsome of this vorticity being sheared away from TS Vance hanging up \njust of the coast of Mexico. Certainly the QPF connection to Vance \nis reflected as there is a large area of elevated QPF moving across \nthe Mexican plateau and to the Rio Grande by early Thursday morning. \nUnfortunately eastern counties will get another round of ESE low \nlevel winds from a persistent surface ridge covering much of the Deep \nSouth, leaving only some spotty rain chances while deeper moisture \ncollects generally west of I-35. Out over the Edwards Plateau a \nsolid training of elevated rain along with some pockets of deeper \nconvection is expected occupy Thursday through midday Friday, with \n90% plus PoPs in for Thursday and Thursday night. The system dumps \nmost of the heavy rains over this higher terrain area and looks to \nbecome less concentrated as the focus expands east to the rest of \nthe CWA Friday. Again this NE ejection pulls the best dynamics out \nof the area before better moisture profiles can get established \nfarther east. Thus our storm total rains for late Wednesday through \nFriday night show a solid area of 1-2, isolated 4 inch QPF for Val \nVerde into parts of Edwards and Kinney Counties, then tapering off \nquickly to below half an inch before getting to the I-35 Corridor \nand Coastal Prairies where only a few hundredths to maybe a quarter \ninch of rain is expected. \n\nIn the wake of the ejecting shortwave, more isolated daytime \nconvection is depicted with the weak pattern aloft Saturday, then \nbecoming more stable as a low amplitude upper ridge starts to build \nover TX again Sunday. \n\nThe late week rain chances will lead to about a 2 to 6 degree \ndowntrend in the max temps for those two days. Outside of that, our \ntemperatures will continue to run mild to warm through Monday before \nour next good cold front arrives possibly Monday night. There could \nbe a convective squall line to accompany that front, but there \nremains considerable model variability that goes into the model \nblends.",
